1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Seven keys sit in height 3 with black-height 2: root 20, children 12 and 30, and 5, 10, 15, 25 below.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Delete the red leaf 25 and no fix-up runs at all: no path lost a black, so all five rules still hold.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Delete the black leaf 15 with the repair skipped and rule five fails at node 12: two blacks on the left, one on the right.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Only rule five can break. The whole lesson repairs one quantity: a single missing black on one path.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Delete 12 and the key that leaves the tree is not 12: 12 has two children, so the successor 15 is the node that moves.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
15 takes 12's place and 12's colour, which was red. The colour travels with the position, not with the key.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The missing black is at 15's old position — its right NIL — so the repair starts under 15, not where 12 stood.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The moved node's old colour decides everything: 15 was black, so a fix-up runs. Had it been red, the deletion would be over.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The fix-up carries one quantity: x is a node one black short, and the debt lives in the loop, not in the node.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
The loop ends in three ways: x is red, so colour it black; x is the root, so drop the debt; or a rotation case finishes.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
x may be the NIL sentinel, so the code keeps x's parent. The sibling is never NIL, because that path would already be short.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The invariant: every path other than the one through x already carries the right number of blacks.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Built by inserting 1 to 6: root 2, left child 1, right child 4 with 3 and 5 below it, and 6 under 5. Now delete 1.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Case 1: the sibling 4 is red. Recolour 4 black and 2 red, then rotate left at 2 — one rotation.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The new sibling is 3, black with two black children, so case 2 fires: recolour 3 red and climb to the parent, which is red.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Case 1 finished nothing. It bought a black sibling and handed over: cases 1 and 2, one rotation, height 3 down to 2.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
An all-black perfect tree of seven keys: root 8, children 4 and 12, and four leaves. Black-height 3, height 2.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Delete the black leaf 2. Its sibling 6 is black and both its children are NIL, so case 2 fires.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Recolour 6 red and move x up to 4. Sibling 12 has black children too, so case 2 fires a second time.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Recolour 12 red and move x to the root, where the debt is dropped: black-height 3 falls to 2, height stays 2.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Built by inserting 1, 2, 4, 3: root 2 black, left child 1 black, right child 4 black with 3 red on its left.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Delete 1. x is its NIL child; the sibling 4 is black, the near nephew 3 is red, the far nephew is NIL.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Case 3: recolour 3 black and 4 red, then rotate right at 4. The far nephew is now red.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Case 4 finishes it: two rotations in all, and the tree drops from height 2 to height 1 with three keys.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Built by inserting 1, 2, 3, 4: root 2 black, left child 1 black, right child 3 black with 4 red on its right.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Delete 1. The sibling 3 is black and the far nephew 4 is red, so case 4 fires at once.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The sibling takes the parent's colour, the parent and the far nephew go black, and one rotation at the parent ends it.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
One rotation and the debt is paid. Case 4 is the only case that ends a deletion by rotating.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The ten-key build of 5, 10, 9, 1, 2, 8, 7, 4, 3, 6 has height 4. Delete 9: cases 2, 3 and 4, and two rotations.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Delete 1: case 2 twice and no rotation. The debt walks up to the root and is dropped there.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Delete 7: no fix-up at all, because the successor 8 was red.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Delete 4: no case either — the successor 5 was black but its child 6 was red, so one recolour absorbs the debt.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The page runs every insertion order of up to six keys and deletes every key from each: 5,039 deletions in all.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
1,080 of them fire at least one case and 3,959 need none. The largest rotation count is 3, seen 108 times.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Only five case sequences occur in the sweep: 4, 1-2, 1-4, 3-4 and 1-3-4. No bare case 1 and no bare case 3.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
So deletion costs O(log n) recolourings and at most three rotations — against AVL deletion, which may rotate at every level.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step 1: splice. With two children the successor moves up, and the key that leaves the tree is the successor.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step 2: colour. The moved node takes the deleted node's colour — and you must save the colour it had before.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step 3: decide. Old colour red, stop. Child red, colour it black and stop. Otherwise the debt is real.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Step 4: repair. Read the sibling, then the two nephews; report the cases in order and count rotations only.

